proof.text:
Define $\alpha_N=G'T(p)\beta_P\,LG(p)^{-1}$, inserting the localization identification $Q_{\mathcal O}M=N$. The resolution-independence theorem states exactly that this expression is unchanged for a different flat-term K-flat sheaf resolution. Step 4 makes it an isomorphism. Naturality of the two units and the equation of step 5 give
$$\operatorname{Res}(\alpha_N)u_{GN}
=G\operatorname{Res}(T(p))G(v_P)G(p)^{-1}
=G(v_N).$$
The normalized extension/restriction adjunction identifies a map $LGN\to G'TN$ with its composite with $u_{GN}$ after restriction, so this equation specifies $\alpha_N$ uniquely. For $f:N\to N'$, the adjuncts of $G'T(f)\alpha_N$ and $\alpha_{N'}LG(f)$ are respectively $G(\operatorname{Res}T(f)\,v_N)$ and $G(v_{N'}f)$; these are equal by naturality of $v$. Injectivity of the adjunction bijection proves naturality for every derived morphism $f$, not merely for chain maps.
